</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/lg-error-codes"
  eyebrow="LG Refrigerator Error Codes · Reference Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="LG Refrigerator Error Codes, Explained"
  subtitle="What FF, dH, CO, and the rest actually mean — which you can clear yourself, which need a tech, and why CL isn't an error at all."
  heroImageTitle="LG Refrigerator Error Codes"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · Start here</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">First: if you see CL, that's not an error.</h2>

    <p><strong>The most common "code" LG owners panic over is CL — and it isn't a fault. CL is the Child Lock indicator: the panel is locked, usually by accident. Hold the Lock button for 3 to 5 seconds and it clears. The real error codes on LG refrigerators are two letters (sometimes shown with an Er, ER, or E prefix that doesn't change the meaning). The most common are FF and rF (fan faults), dH (defrost), CO (communication), and the sensor codes FS, rS, and dS. Many fan and communication codes clear with a power cycle or a defrost; sensor codes and any code that keeps coming back need a tech. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>One important distinction up front: plain <strong>CL</strong> is the child lock, but <strong>CL-E</strong> and <strong>CH-E</strong> are different and serious — they point to a refrigerant (sealed-system) problem and are not something to clear yourself. If it's just CL with a locked panel, press the lock button. If it's CL-E or CH-E, that's a diagnostic.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 FAN + DEFROST CODES -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Fan and defrost codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The cooling and airflow faults.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>FF — freezer evaporator fan</h3>
      <p>The freezer fan isn't running normally. Often the motor failed, or frost has locked the blade. Try a 5-minute power cycle; if frost-locked, a full defrost clears it. Returns after that = tech.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>rF — refrigerator (fresh-food) fan</h3>
      <p>The fridge-side fan motor isn't running properly. Power-cycle first; a persistent rF means the fan motor needs replacing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>IF / 1F — ice-maker fan</h3>
      <p>The ice-maker fan has failed or iced up. Empty the ice bin and let the compartment defrost; if it returns, the fan or a sensor is the cause.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>CF — condenser fan</h3>
      <p>The condenser fan (which sheds the compressor's heat, usually at the rear or bottom) isn't giving a feedback signal. It's behind a panel — a tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>dH — defrost fault</h3>
      <p>The defrost cycle ran too long without finishing, pointing to a failed defrost heater, sensor, or thermal fuse. One power cycle is worth a try; a returning dH is a diagnostic.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 SENSOR + COMMS + WATER CODES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Sensor, communication, and water codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The rest of the common ones.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>FS, rS, dS — temperature and defrost sensors</h3>
      <p>FS is the freezer temperature sensor, rS the fridge temperature sensor, and dS the defrost sensor, each signaling a short or disconnection. These are failed-part codes with no homeowner fix — they need a tech to test and replace the thermistor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>CO — communication error</h3>
      <p>The main control board and the display board have lost communication, often from a power blip or a loose harness. Unplug the fridge for 60 seconds and restore power. If CO persists, it's wiring or a board.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>gF — water flow sensor</h3>
      <p>The fridge isn't sensing enough water flow to the dispenser or ice maker. Check that the water supply valve is fully open and the line isn't kinked. If the supply is fine, the flow sensor is the fault.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>It — ice-maker fault · HS — humidity sensor</h3>
      <p>It points to the ice-maker motor or assembly; HS is a humidity-sensor fault (rare). Both are part-level and are tech replacements.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>CL — child lock (not an error) · CL-E / CH-E — refrigerant</h3>
      <p>Plain CL is the child lock — hold the Lock button to clear. CL-E and CH-E are separate, serious sealed-system (refrigerant) codes and need a tech; do not treat them as a lock issue.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely try</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Before you call, run these.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Check for child lock first.</strong> If the panel shows CL and won't respond, hold the Lock button 3–5 seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug the fridge for 60 seconds (5 minutes for fan codes) and restart. This clears CO and transient glitches.</li>
      <li><strong>Full defrost for frost-locked fan codes.</strong> For FF, IF, or 1F, unplug the unit and let it thaw fully with doors open — about a day — to melt frost off the fan.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the water supply for gF.</strong> Confirm the supply valve is open and the line isn't kinked.</li>
      <li><strong>Note whether the code returns.</strong> A code that clears and stays gone was a glitch. A code that comes back is a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> child-lock release, power cycling, defrost thawing, and checking the water supply are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system (any CL-E/CH-E refrigerant code), test the sensors or boards, or pull the rear evaporator panel yourself. Sensor and sealed-system codes are tech work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A code that keeps returning means a failed part.</h2>

    <p>When a power cycle and a defrost don't hold — or the code is a sensor (FS, rS, dS, HS), a condenser fan (CF), an ice-maker fault (It), or a refrigerant code (CL-E/CH-E) — the part has to be tested and replaced. Our techs carry the common LG fan motors, defrost components, and thermistors, so most LG code calls are a one-visit fix. We verify the simple causes first (frost, a loose harness, a closed water valve) before quoting a part.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/">refrigerator repair</a> hub, and if the fridge simply isn't cooling, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a>. Samsung owners: see our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/samsung-error-codes/">Samsung refrigerator error codes</a> guide. Brand coverage on <a href="/brands/lg-refrigerator-repair/">LG refrigerator repair</a> and the <a href="/brands/lg/">LG appliance repair</a> pillar. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG refrigerator codes: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>LG code back after a reset? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

